.\" ABSD manual page source (mdoc(7)); rendered by scripts/render-man.sh.
.Dd November 4, 2026
.Dt ABSD-SH 1
.Os ABSD
.Sh NAME
.Nm absd-sh
.Nd native ABSD command shell and script interpreter
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl c Ar command Op Ar name Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Fl s
.Op Ar argument ...
.Nm
.Ar file
.Op Ar argument ...
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the native ABSD shell, also installed as
.Pa /bin/sh .
Without arguments it reads interactive commands with the prompt
.Ql absd$ .
An unfinished quote or compound command continues at
.Ql > .
Interactive reads stop at each command newline, leaving following pasted bytes
in the inherited input pipe for a foreground application such as cat.
Script input retains its existing buffered parsing behavior.
.Fl c
executes command text, with the optional name as $0 and following arguments
as $1 and later parameters.
.Fl s
reads a script from standard input without prompts.
Otherwise the named file is read as a script; $0 is its name.
.Pp
The upstream conch-parser grammar handles shell syntax.
Evaluation uses ABSD process handles and explicit stream and filesystem
transfer, without fork or Unix job control.
Every directly spawned native command loses the spawn right.
Interpreted scripts run inside the authority-holding shell and can compose
native children, all supervised by that shell.
.Ss Words and expansions
Single quotes preserve literal text, including dollar signs.
Double quotes preserve spaces while allowing variable and command expansion.
Backslash quotes the next character outside single quotes.
A backslash followed by newline continues a command.
Comments start with an unquoted # at a word boundary.
Carriage return (CR) is a literal word byte, including outside quotes;
it is not shell whitespace. CRLF scripts therefore retain CR in command
names, arguments, assignments and redirection paths, as in Bash, and can
fail to find commands or delimiters. For example, echo x > f followed by
CR and newline writes to a filename ending in CR, not to f.
Terminal input configured to translate CR to newline does so before parsing.
.Pp
Assignments, export and unset manage shell variables.
Unexported variables stay inside the shell.
Assignments preceding a native command apply to that command's environment;
the previous shell values are restored afterward.
Export assignment arguments preserve spaces.
Supported parameters include $name, ${name}, $?, $0, $1 and later positions,
$#, $@, $*, $$ and ${#name}.
Quoted "$@" preserves individual argument boundaries, including empty ones.
Default, alternative, assignment and required-value forms are supported:
${name:-word}, ${name:+word}, ${name:=word}, ${name:?message},
and their forms without a colon.
.Pp
Unquoted expansions split on space, tab and newline.
IFS is not consulted; assigning IFS does not change this fixed splitting rule.
Filename patterns *, ? and bracket expressions expand in sorted order;
a leading dot must be explicit and unmatched patterns remain literal.
Quoted or escaped pattern characters remain literal.
A leading unquoted tilde expands HOME; named-user tildes are not implemented.
An SSH login shell starts in writable /home/operator.
SSH command requests start in /, including requests interpreted by the shell;
use cd explicitly when a command needs a writable working directory.
.Pp
Commands are looked up in /bin, or relative to the shell's working directory
when their name contains a slash. PATH is passed to children but is not a
shell search-path configuration.
.Pp
Command substitutions $(commands) and backticks collect standard output,
remove trailing newlines and preserve shell variables, arguments and cwd.
The output limit is 1 MiB; overflow is an error.
Arithmetic expansion supports integers, variables, +, -, *, /, %,
parentheses and unary +, - and ! with checked overflow and division.
.Ss Commands and control flow
Pipelines connect native commands; an output builtin may start a pipeline.
The pipeline status is its last command's status.
Lists support semicolon, newline, &&, || and pipeline negation !.
Control flow includes if/elif/else, for, while, until, case,
brace groups, isolated parenthesized groups and function definitions.
Break and continue operate on the innermost loop without numeric arguments.
Requests made in while or until guards belong to that loop and are consumed
before the surrounding command list resumes. The guard's normal terminating
condition takes precedence over a pending request.
Functions and parenthesized groups start with their own loop context.
Outside an active loop, zero-argument break and continue print a diagnostic,
return status 0 and allow following commands to run.
Functions support positional arguments but no return builtin.
.Pp
Builtins are cd, pwd, echo (including -n), exit, status, help,
true, false, :, export, unset, set --, shift, break, continue,
sh and dot (source).
Plain ps asks the session server for this session's process list;
ps with arguments invokes /bin/ps when that command is installed.
The shell does not supply machine-wide process inspection itself.
Shutdown, reboot and clock ask the session server, which applies its own authority
and command policy.
See clock(1) for boot-local civil time and configured synchronization.
A shell without session control cannot make those requests.
.Ss Scripts and files
Use sh FILE ARG... or ./FILE ARG... .
A directly invoked script must begin with exactly
.Ql #!/bin/sh
or
.Ql #!/bin/absd-sh .
The shebang interpreter cannot have flags.
A file explicitly passed to sh may omit the shebang.
Dot FILE executes in the current shell environment.
Syntax and unsupported structural forms are validated across the whole
parsed script before any command or redirection runs, including dead branches.
A shell parsing or evaluation error inside sh, a direct script or dot
becomes status 2 for that
invocation; the caller may handle it with if, && or ||.
Errors whose command names depend on expansion are diagnosed when expanded.
A script run with sh or a direct path has its own variable, argument,
function and working-directory scope.
.Pp
Native commands accept < FILE, > FILE, >> FILE, 2> FILE and 2>> FILE.
Output descriptor duplication supports literal descriptors 1 and 2:
2>&1 sends stderr to the current stdout destination, and 1>&2 sends stdout
to the current stderr destination.
Redirection operands are expanded before files are opened; output opens
and duplications then apply from left to right.
A duplication keeps the destination selected at that point.
.Pp
With > FILE 2>&1 both streams share one opened file.
With 2>&1 > FILE stderr keeps the original stdout destination while stdout
goes to FILE.
Repeated output opens retain their creation/truncation effects.
When both native streams share a pipe-backed destination, the child gets
two copies of one writer endpoint, preserving its stream-write order.
A pipeline may receive stderr after stdout is redirected elsewhere;
a pipe whose writer was redirected away reaches end of input.
.Pp
The shell opens files and copies between them and child pipes;
children do not receive the shell's open filesystem objects.
A native command's own diagnostics follow its stderr destination.
When its stderr has an explicit redirection, or is inherited from a
redirected surrounding group, the shell omits its extra exit-status line.
The shell also omits that extra line when testing a status in an if/elif,
while or until guard, under pipeline negation, or before a following
&& or || operand. Functions, scripts, groups, subshells and command
substitutions inherit that condition context. The bodies of an if or loop
construct also inherit it when the whole construct is evaluated as a condition.
Actual command diagnostics remain visible, and the numeric status is still
available through status and $?.
A final operand outside a condition retains ordinary status reporting.
When reported without stderr redirection, the extra exit-status line uses
the enclosing shell error destination.
Preparation, start and stream-copy errors still use that enclosing destination.
Output builtins and interpreted groups support output/error redirection.
Session/control builtins such as cd and exit still refuse their own
redirections, and output builtins at the head of a pipeline take no
redirections.
Input redirection on an interpreted script or group is refused; redirect
the native command that consumes the file instead.
File input redirection reads at most 4 KiB into a pending buffer, then
feeds the child's stdin through a pipe in steps of at most 256 bytes.
Short writes and timeouts retain the pending suffix; each step yields to
other streams and session control. A synchronous filesystem read can still
delay the next control check. Direct filename arguments let a command read
the file itself, without this shell-to-stdin copy.
The prefixes ro and nofs give native commands read-only or no filesystem
authority. They do not apply to interpreted builtins or scripts.
File output uses one shared 4 KiB buffer per opened endpoint, including
stdout/stderr aliases. The copier flushes pending bytes when full, when its
pipe is idle, or after 16 monotonic ticks between copier steps.
Filesystem waits can extend that interval. End of output and Ctrl-C flush
accepted bytes and request durability. The copier's session END cleanup
flushes accepted bytes without adding a sync request; enclosing file scopes
and file close may still sync. Write and flush failures are reported, and
failed buffered suffixes are not silently retried on close.
.Pp
For supported redirections only, the exact expanded word /dev/null is
provided by the shell: output is discarded, and input is immediate end
of input.
No filesystem entry or device is created, and no filesystem access is
needed for that endpoint.
Both > /dev/null and >> /dev/null discard bytes.
Normal alias ordering still applies: > /dev/null 2>&1 discards both streams,
while 2>&1 > /dev/null leaves stderr on the earlier stdout destination.
Other spellings are ordinary paths.
This is not a general /dev filesystem: a program that opens /dev/null by
name, such as cat /dev/null, still fails because that file does not exist
in the session.
.Ss Heredocs
Native consuming commands accept <<WORD and <<-WORD on standard input
(descriptor 0).
The nonempty delimiter ends the body on a line of its own.
Quoting any part of the delimiter keeps the body literal; otherwise
parameters, command substitutions and arithmetic expand as text without
field splitting, globbing or tilde expansion.
Quote characters inside an unquoted body are ordinary text.
Backslash preserves its byte before a double quote; backslash-newline
joins body lines before delimiter matching. A delimiter can span joined
physical lines; a delimiter-looking suffix after a joined prefix stays body.
Backslash escapes dollar, backtick and backslash.
Quoted delimiters disable this joining and keep body backslashes literal.
The <<- form strips leading tab characters from body and delimiter lines;
spaces and tabs after a joined nonempty prefix are retained.
Carriage return is a literal delimiter or body byte. A terminal configured
to translate CR to newline performs that translation before shell parsing.
An empty body delivers end of input.
.Pp
The shell feeds the body through the existing native input pipe, without
creating a temporary file or granting filesystem authority.
The expanded body is limited to 1 MiB; overflow fails before its consumer
starts.
This bounds the delivered body, not every allocation in an existing
parameter or command substitution.
Duplicate input redirections, input on a later pipeline stage, and heredocs
on interpreted commands or builtins remain unsupported.
A command name determined by expansion may run body substitutions before
being refused as an interpreted command; pre-execution structural refusal
applies to forms and names known while validating the input.
Interactive input continues at the > prompt until the delimiter arrives;
Ctrl-C cancels the accumulated input and its pending delimiter.
A bracketed paste may contain the whole heredoc or finish one already being
collected, followed by more commands; press Enter after the paste to submit it.
Large unbracketed input batches can be echoed before the shell writes their
continuation prompts. Repeated > prompts can then appear together, with the
next absd$ prompt on the same displayed line. Prompt placement does not mark
an input or command boundary; this cosmetic limitation does not change the
heredoc delimiter rules.
A missing delimiter at script or command EOF reports an unterminated-heredoc
error before any command in that input runs.
A delimiter at EOF needs no final newline.
The parser reports the pending delimiter and tab mode; ordinary body lines
are collected without reparsing the whole accumulated body each time.
.Pp
Compatibility bound: parameter-default words use this shell's ordinary word quoting;
${UNSET:-'c'} produces c, without the single quotes.
This differs from dash. Use a quoted delimiter for literal body bytes.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cd /home/operator
cat <<'TEXT' > notes.txt
$HOME stays literal
TEXT
cat notes.txt
NAME='field tech'
cat <<TEXT
operator: $NAME
sum: $((2 + 3))
TEXT
.Ed
.Ss SSH command routing
The stock SSH server uses argv mode for exec requests. Text with
shell-significant syntax (operators, quotes, escapes, expansions or patterns),
or starting with an assignment or a control/script builtin such as cd, export,
if or sh, runs through absd-sh -c. That text is interpreted
with the same command permissions as an interactive shell; quoted or patterned
arguments are not passed as uninterpreted words. Plain native commands such as
cat FILE retain direct execution.
.Ss SSH terminal editing and history
SSH PTY terminals support left and right arrows, Home, End and Delete.
Backspace removes the preceding character, Ctrl-W the preceding word and
Ctrl-U the current line. Tabs are stored but displayed as ^I.
Editing handles single-column UTF-8 characters; wide and combining character
layout and wrapped-line cursor motion are not implemented.
PTY input must be valid UTF-8. A line longer than 65536 bytes or invalid
UTF-8 is refused; subsequent input is discarded until bounded recovery
completes. In a controlled interactive shell session, the SSH connection
remains open and recovery uses the fresh code shown by the terminal,
followed by Enter. Ordinary keys and Ctrl-C cannot release refused input.
A direct command PTY recovers through the same displayed code; its SSH
connection owns the input pause. Explicit client EOF or disconnect retains
its normal meaning.
This also applies to input to applications such as ed. Non-PTY input is
unaffected; use a non-PTY SSH stream for byte-preserving file transfers.
.Pp
A new bracketed paste first classified for the shell is staged until its closing marker and
an explicit Enter. Newlines inside that paste do not execute commands.
Review the text before pressing Enter. The physical editing-line limit is
65536 bytes; the complete staged source is bounded below 1 MiB.
A refused bracketed paste is discarded through its closing marker; a missing
marker can be recovered using the displayed fresh code and Enter.
Ctrl-C before approval cancels the capture without executing its suffix.
These markers are in-band text: a clipboard containing a forged closing
marker can release later commands. Bracketed paste does not make clipboard
content trustworthy. A complete line arriving after the real closing marker
and recovery can likewise be treated as fresh typing and execute.
A partially discarded line remains discarded through its newline.
If a completed line is discarded after recovery, the terminal reports it and
shows the current shell prompt again. Enter that command again.
.Pp
Application-owned paste streams through the existing cooked input processing,
including newlines and Ctrl-C, without waiting for shell approval. Ownership
at the opening marker persists through the closing marker across prompt
changes. If an application enabled bracketed paste with ?2004h, its markers
are forwarded; otherwise the markers are stripped. The 65536-byte physical
line bound, UTF-8 checking and canonical editing still apply.
Ctrl-C in an open application paste shows bounded refusal and a recovery code
if its closing marker is missing; direct exec retains ordinary Ctrl-C termination.
A stray application opening marker can keep the next paste application-owned
after prompt return, so its lines stream without shell approval. Use the
visible Ctrl-C recovery before starting a fresh shell paste in that state.
Only standalone ?2004h/l requests update marker forwarding; combined mode
sequences do not update that selection.
A paste queued behind
an earlier typed-ahead command that reads inherited stdin can be consumed by
that command, including internal framing bytes. Wait for the actual shell
prompt before pasting shell source. Ordinary input intended for an application
should be sent after that application starts.
.Pp
Up and down arrows recall up to 32 nonempty primary-prompt lines in this session,
within a 128 KiB history-text budget;
down past the newest entry restores the current draft.
History is held in memory by the terminal service, has no history file,
and ends with logout or disconnection. Consecutive duplicate lines use one
entry.
.Pp
Login tokens, script input and application input are never collected in history.
The shell explicitly enables history while reading a primary prompt and disables
it before running the line. Submitted lines immediately disarm collection,
even when further application input arrives in the same network batch.
Prompt-mode changes preserve unfinished bytes, cursor and partial UTF-8/escape
sequences within the same live session. Any draft accepting input outside an
armed primary prompt stays ineligible for history until completed or cancelled;
rearming the prompt and recalling history cannot promote that draft.
During a fast paste, a continuation prompt can appear within text already
echoed on the screen; that displayed prompt is not part of the input bytes.
Unfinished application text may therefore remain pending and become executable
shell input after prompt return, including text a child with inherited stdin
did not read. Mode changes do not prove private consumption or purge that text.
Complete lines already submitted to the input pipe remain queued as before.
Ctrl-C cancels the unfinished draft and pending complete input, including
bytes already forwarded to the shell. Further bytes are discarded until
the shell finishes cancellation and prints a fresh prompt. This cannot undo
commands that executed before cancellation. EOF and session retirement
retain their existing behavior.
.Pp
Input queues are bounded. A foreground program that does not read stdin
can fill those queues; Ctrl-C waiting behind that input is not observed
until space becomes available. Disconnect that SSH session if it cannot
drain.
.Pp
Use terminal-size to query observed dimensions or terminal-size --watch
to observe SSH window changes. Unknown serial dimensions remain unknown.
TERM, COLUMNS and LINES describe the observed terminal when available;
COLUMNS and LINES refresh at each shell prompt and native command start.
No termios, raw mode, fullscreen editor or job control is implied.
The installed ed editor uses these canonical input semantics.
Ctrl-D after partial prompt input sends those bytes without a newline;
the prompt remains active, so Ctrl-C still wakes and cancels the unfinished read.
Middle-line pastes redraw the accepted insertion as a batch.
A malformed or refused SSH resize keeps the previous known dimensions
and leaves the session running.
.Ss Interrupt and bounds
At a prompt, Ctrl-C cancels the whole unfinished command, including earlier
continuation lines, and restores the primary prompt with status 130.
In a managed session, Ctrl-C ends and reaps the shell's running children.
Builtin loops poll the same control channel and stop with status 130.
Each builtin command performs a timed control check.
The one-tick timeout can span two timer periods before scheduling delay;
it is not a wall-clock response guarantee.
Native redirection copying continues to check control events while bytes flow,
without waiting on an empty event channel when a copy round made progress.
Idle commands retain timed control checks.
Loss of an established control channel ends the managed session and reaps
its native children, as an explicit session END does.
Session end also ends children before the shell exits.
There is no background-job syntax, fork, Unix signals or process groups.
.Pp
A script or continued interactive command is limited to 1 MiB.
On the managed local console, unterminated input past that limit refuses the
unfinished command once and discards its pending suffix until explicit Ctrl-C.
The session survives and accepts a separate command after its fresh prompt.
Newlines and heredoc delimiters cannot release refused input.
SSH without a PTY still ends the shell session at that source limit.
The SSH PTY path retains its terminal recovery protocol.
Evaluation is limited to 64 nesting levels and 100000 commands per input;
exceeding a limit reports an error rather than continuing indefinitely.
These are operating limits, not a complete POSIX compatibility claim.
.Ss Unsupported forms
Unsupported forms report a diagnostic and status 2:
background jobs, descriptor closure (such as 2>&-),
input descriptor duplication, output descriptor numbers other than 1 and 2,
nonliteral descriptor-duplication targets,
script builtins/functions/compound commands inside native pipelines,
compound input redirection, parameter pattern removal, unsupported
arithmetic operators, shell options beyond set --, numeric break/continue,
return, read, trap, exec, eval, wait, local, getopts, ulimit, umask,
jobs, fg and bg.
These reserved unsupported builtin names fail with status 2 rather than
falling through to an executable lookup.
Unknown command names follow ordinary executable lookup and report 127 if
missing.
The test command is the native
.Pa /bin/test ;
it is not a shell builtin.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Create a script in a writable directory with the line editor:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cd /home/operator
ed -p ': ' notes.sh
a
#!/bin/sh
for item in one 'two words'; do
    echo "$item"
done
\&.
w
q
sh notes.sh
.Ed
.Pp
Inspect and update a file without shell escapes inside the editor:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
ed -p ': ' notes.sh
H
1,$p
s/one/first/
u
s/one/first/
w
q
.Ed
.Pp
Capture a diagnostic and check its status:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
net-status > network.txt
if test -s network.txt; then
    summary=$(head -n 1 network.txt)
    echo "$summary"
fi
.Ed
.Pp
Capture both streams, then keep a diagnostic on the original stdout while
saving only ordinary output. The missing file deliberately produces a
nonzero command status and diagnostic:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
cd /home/operator
{ /bin/echo output; /bin/cat /missing; } > combined.txt 2>&1
cat combined.txt
{ /bin/echo output; /bin/cat /missing; } 2>&1 > output.txt
cat output.txt
.Ed
.Pp
Discard a native diagnostic while retaining its status, or provide empty
input without requiring a file:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
/bin/cat /missing 2>/dev/null
status
/bin/cat </dev/null
.Ed
The first command has a nonzero numeric status but prints no native
diagnostic or extra shell exit-status line.
.Sh DIAGNOSTICS
Zero indicates success; a native command's nonzero status is preserved.
A failed native command with unredirected stderr may print an exit-status
diagnostic in addition to its own standard error.
Shell syntax or unsupported forms return 2; redirection failure returns 1;
missing executables return 127; refused starts return 126;
abnormal termination returns 128 and managed interruption returns 130.
